---
// The galvanic cell at a joint, and the effect of the area ratio.
const ink = 'var(--rd-ink)', muted = 'var(--rd-muted)', steel = 'var(--rd-steel)', hot = 'var(--rd-accent)', blue = 'var(--rd-blue)';
const halo = { stroke: 'var(--rd-panel)', 'stroke-width': 4, 'paint-order': 'stroke', 'stroke-linejoin': 'round' };
const arrowsL = [60, 95, 130]; // current from a small cathode spreads over a large anode
const arrowsR = [210, 235, 290, 315]; // current from a large cathode goes into a small anode
---

<figure class="viz gcell">
  <p class="viz-title">The galvanic cell</p>
  <svg viewBox="0 0 360 260" role="img" aria-labelledby="gcell-desc" style="max-width: 520px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <desc id="gcell-desc">
      Top: an aluminium plate and a steel plate in contact, with a film of water over the joint. Electrons go through the
      metal contact from the aluminium to the steel. The aluminium dissolves at the anode, and oxygen is reduced at the
      steel cathode. Bottom left: a small steel bolt head in a large aluminium plate, with the current spread over a large
      anode area, so the attack is mild. Bottom right: a small aluminium rivet head in a large steel plate, with all the
      current from the large cathode going into the small anode, so the attack is severe.
    </desc>
    <defs>
      <marker id="gc-arrow" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,0 L10,5 L0,10 z" fill={hot}></path></marker>
    </defs>
    <path d="M50,78 Q180,22 310,78 Z" fill={blue} fill-opacity="0.25" stroke={blue}></path>
    <text x="180" y="62" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={blue}>water film</text>
    <rect x="20" y="78" width="160" height="22" fill={hot} fill-opacity="0.45"></rect>
    <rect x="180" y="78" width="160" height="22" fill={steel} fill-opacity="0.7"></rect>
    <line x1="150" x2="210" y1="89" y2="89" stroke={ink} stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#gc-arrow)"></line>
    <text x="128" y="94" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill={ink}>e⁻</text>
    <text x="24" y="118" font-size="13" fill={hot}>anode: Al</text>
    <text x="336" y="118" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill={ink}>cathode: steel</text>
    <text x="24" y="138" font-size="13" fill={muted}>Al → Al³⁺ + 3e⁻</text>
    <text x="336" y="138" text-anchor="end" font-size="13" fill={muted}>O₂ + 2H₂O + 4e⁻ → 4OH⁻</text>

    <rect x="20" y="198" width="150" height="14" fill={hot} fill-opacity="0.45"></rect>
    <rect x="85" y="188" width="20" height="10" fill={steel}></rect>
    {arrowsL.map((x) => <line x1="95" x2={x} y1="186" y2="196" stroke={hot} stroke-width="1.5" marker-end="url(#gc-arrow)" opacity="0.6"></line>)}
    <rect x="190" y="198" width="150" height="14" fill={steel} fill-opacity="0.7"></rect>
    <rect x="255" y="188" width="20" height="10" fill={hot}></rect>
    {arrowsR.map((x) => <line x1={x} x2={x < 265 ? 256 : 274} y1="176" y2="188" stroke={hot} stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#gc-arrow)"></line>)}
    <text x="95" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={ink} {...halo}>small cathode</text>
    <text x="95" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={muted}>mild attack</text>
    <text x="265" y="232" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={ink} {...halo}>small anode</text>
    <text x="265" y="250" text-anchor="middle" font-size="13" fill={hot}>severe attack</text>
  </svg>
  <figcaption>
    Three things make a galvanic cell: two different metals, an electrical contact between them, and an electrolyte that
    touches both. Remove one of the three and the cell stops. The cathode area sets the current, and the anode area
    carries it. A small anode next to a large cathode corrodes fast.
  </figcaption>
</figure>
